{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":"2026-10-23T04:01:35.682Z","tool_name":"run_shell_command","tool_id":"run_shell_command__call_768672","parameters":{"command":"python3 -c '\nimport itertools\n\ndef dominates(x, y):\n    return (x[0] > y[0] and x[1] > y[1]) or (x[0] > y[0] and x[2] > y[2]) or (x[1] > y[1] and x[2] > y[2])\n\nfor K in range(2, 12):\n    pts = [(a, b, K - a - b) for a in range(K + 1) for b in range(K + 1 - a)]\n    stable_sets = []\n    # Any internally stable set is either a subset of a line x_i = c, or a triangle of 3 points!\n    # Wait: let us test ALL subsets for K <= 5, and for larger K test all candidate maximal antichains and subsets!\n    if K <= 5:\n        # find all stable sets by checking all cliques in incomparability graph\n        pass\n    # Wait: if V is internally stable, CAN a non-maximal internally stable set be externally stable?\n    # No! Because if V is externally stable, every y not in V is dominated by some x in V, so V CANNOT be extended to a larger internally stable set!\n    # Wait: every stable set is a MAXIMAL internally stable set (maximal antichain / kernel), AND wait: in fact, since x and y are incomparable iff neither dominates the other, wait:\n    # If y is not in V and y is not dominated by any x in V, does y dominate something in V, or is y incomparable to all of V?\n    # Wait: domination is NOT symmetric, so an externally stable set is a dominating set in the directed graph!\n'","description":"Run quick check in Python"}}
